<ServiceLayout
  title="Estate Planning After Divorce or Loss | Single Again | Mesa Estate Planning"
  description="Divorce or the death of a spouse requires a complete estate plan overhaul. Beneficiary sweeps, agent updates, and new trust structures for Arizona residents starting over."
  heroTitle="Your life changed. Your estate plan didn't."
  heroSubtitle="After a divorce or the loss of a spouse, nearly every document in your estate plan needs to be reviewed — and most of them need to be changed. The sooner you act, the less risk your family carries."
  breadcrumbParent="Life Stages"
  breadcrumbParentUrl="/life-stages"
  keywords="estate planning after divorce arizona, estate plan update after spouse death, beneficiary designation divorce, estate planning widow widower mesa az"
  relatedServices={[
    { title: 'Wills & Trusts', href: '/services/wills-and-trusts', desc: 'New trust and will structures for your new situation.' },
    { title: 'Powers of Attorney', href: '/services/financial-power-of-attorney', desc: 'Replace former spouse as your agent.' },
    { title: 'Healthcare Directive', href: '/services/healthcare-directive', desc: 'Update your healthcare agent and medical wishes.' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="quick-answer">
    <div class="quick-answer-label">Quick Answer</div>
    <p>Divorce and the death of a spouse are two of the most critical triggers for an estate plan review. After a divorce, Arizona law (A.R.S. § 14-2804) automatically revokes certain provisions naming your former spouse — but it doesn't cover everything, especially beneficiary designations on retirement accounts and life insurance. After the death of a spouse, your entire plan needs restructuring: trusts may need to be split or restated, agents need to be replaced, and your own plan needs to reflect your new situation as a single person.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <p>Whether your marriage ended through divorce or the death of your spouse, the legal reality is the same: the estate plan you built as a couple no longer works. Every document references a person who is either no longer your partner or no longer alive. Your agents, trustees, beneficiaries, guardianship designations, and asset distribution plans all need to be reconsidered.</p>

    <p>This is emotional work, and we approach it with sensitivity. But it's also urgent work. Every day that passes with outdated documents is a day your family is exposed to outcomes you didn't intend.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>After Divorce: What Changes</h2>
    <p><strong>What Arizona Law Does Automatically.</strong> Under A.R.S. § 14-2804, a divorce automatically revokes provisions in your will and trust that benefit your former spouse — including their role as beneficiary, trustee, agent, or guardian. This is helpful, but it's not complete protection.</p>

    <p><strong>What Arizona Law Does NOT Change.</strong> Beneficiary designations on retirement accounts (401(k)s, IRAs), life insurance policies, and payable-on-death bank accounts are governed by federal law or contract law — not by Arizona's automatic revocation statute. If your ex-spouse is still named as beneficiary on your 401(k), they will receive those funds when you die, regardless of your divorce decree or your will. This is one of the most common and costly mistakes in post-divorce estate planning.</p>

    <p><strong>Your Action Items After Divorce.</strong> Update every beneficiary designation on every account. Execute a new will or trust that reflects your current wishes. Name new agents on your financial power of attorney and healthcare directive. If you have children, review and update guardianship designations. Review your life insurance — both the beneficiary and whether the coverage amount still makes sense.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>After the Death of a Spouse: What Changes</h2>
    <p><strong>Trust Administration.</strong> If you had a joint revocable trust, it likely needs to be split into a survivor's trust and a decedent's trust, or restated entirely. The provisions that applied to a married couple no longer apply to a surviving single person.</p>

    <p><strong>New Agents and Trustees.</strong> If your deceased spouse was your healthcare agent, financial power of attorney agent, or successor trustee, those roles are now vacant. You need to name new people immediately.</p>

    <p><strong>Beneficiary Updates.</strong> Accounts that named your deceased spouse as beneficiary may need to be updated to name your trust, your children, or other beneficiaries.</p>

    <p><strong>New Distribution Plan.</strong> Your original plan likely distributed assets as a couple — everything to the surviving spouse, then to children. Now you're the last parent. Your plan needs to address what happens directly to your children, including guardianship if they're minors, trust provisions, and distribution timing.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>What It Costs</h2>
    <p>Post-divorce and post-loss planning is flat-fee, quoted after a <a href="/contact">free consultation</a>. We assess what you currently have, identify what needs to change, and provide a clear scope and price before you commit.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="content-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-section">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          Does my divorce automatically remove my ex from my estate plan?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Partially. Arizona law revokes provisions in your will and trust that benefit your former spouse. But it does not change beneficiary designations on retirement accounts, life insurance, or payable-on-death accounts. Those must be updated manually — and if you don't, your ex-spouse may still receive those assets.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          How soon after a divorce should I update my estate plan?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Immediately. Arizona's automatic revocation provides some protection, but the gaps — particularly around beneficiary designations — are significant. We recommend updating your plan within 30 days of your divorce being finalized. If you're currently going through a divorce, we can begin planning now so documents are ready to execute as soon as the decree is entered.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          My spouse recently passed. Do I need an entirely new estate plan?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>In most cases, yes. A plan built for a married couple doesn't function the same way for a surviving single person. Your trust may need to be restated, your agents need to be replaced, and your distribution plan needs to be redesigned for your current situation. We handle this process with care and walk you through each decision.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-btn" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
          What if I'm thinking about remarrying?
          <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>If remarriage is on the horizon, your estate plan should account for it — especially if you have children from your prior marriage. A <a href="/life-stages/blended-families">blended family plan</a> with a QTIP trust or similar structure protects your children while providing for your new spouse. Planning before the remarriage gives you the most flexibility, including the option of a prenuptial agreement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    function toggleFaq(btn) {
      const item = btn.closest('.faq-item');
      const isOpen = item.classList.contains('open');
      document.querySelectorAll('.faq-item.open').forEach(el => el.classList.remove('open'));
      if (!isOpen) item.classList.add('open');
    }
  </script>
</ServiceLayout>
